# PySide6 Project Rules

## 1) UI-First Workflow (Mandatory)
- All user-facing UI must be designed in Qt Designer first and saved as raw `.ui` files (no Python slots in `.ui`).
- Keep `.ui` files as the single source of truth for layout and widget tree.
- After any `.ui` change, compile UI to Python with:
  ```bash
  python scripts/compile_ui.py
  ```
- Never hand-edit generated UI Python files; edit only the corresponding controller/component files.

## 2) Architecture & Responsibilities
- Controllers extend `core.BaseController` and only wire UI to events via `slot_map`.
    + Inherits orders: 1st: UI class, 2nd: BaseController
    + Never call `setupUi` in `__init__`, cause `BaseController` was called in super().__init__()
- Handlers extend `core.Observer.Subscriber` and implement event logic (`on<event_name_camel_case>` methods). Keep business logic out of controllers.
- Services contain pure, UI-agnostic business logic. Prefer dependency injection and testability.
- Components live under `windows/components/<ComponentName>/` with this structure:
  - `ComponentName.ui` (designer)
  - `ComponentName.py` (generated; do not edit)
  - `ComponentNameWidget.py` (Controller/Widget)
  - `ComponentNameHandler.py` (Handler)
  - `__init__.py`
- Use `WidgetManager` for widget access and signal-suppressed updates.
- Use the Observer (Publisher/Subscriber) for decoupled communication. Do not directly call other controllers.

## 3) Coding Style
- Follow PEP 8/PEP 257 except naming to align with Qt conventions:
  - Classes/Files: PascalCase (e.g., `MainController.py`)
  - Methods/variables: camelCase
  - Directories: lowercase with underscores (e.g., `windows/main`)
- Maximize readability: small methods, clear names, early returns, no deep nesting.
- Type hints required for public functions/methods.
- Errors must use `core.Exceptions` types; do not catch silently.

## 4) Logging & Diagnostics
- Use `core.Logging.logger` everywhere (no `print`).
- Log key start/end points of important flows, include contextual data (avoid secrets).
- In async/multithreaded contexts, include identifiers (task id, thread info) in logs when available.

## 5) ACK Mechanism (If coordination is needed)
- When a task emits events that require downstream processing before cleanup, use the ACK mechanism per `docs/provided-by-base/ACKNOWLEDGMENT_MECHANISM.md`:
  - Emit events via `TaskServiceWithAck.emit_event_with_ack(...)`.
  - Handlers process with `HandlerServiceWithAck.handle_with_ack(...)`.
  - Task services must `wait_for_acknowledgments(...)` before resource cleanup (e.g., closing browser).
- Share a single `AcknowledgmentTracker` between sender/receiver in the same workflow.

## 6) Testing
- Use the provided pytest setup in `tests/` (see `TESTING_SETUP.md`).
- Write unit tests for core logic (services, ack layer) and integration tests for critical flows.
- Commands:
  ```bash
  pytest
  pytest --cov=core --cov=services --cov-report=html
  ```

## 7) Scripts & Scaffolding
- Generate controllers/services/components via `scripts/generate.py` when possible to ensure consistency.
- Re-run `scripts/compile_ui.py` after adding/editing `.ui` files.

## 8) File/Module Conventions
- Keep controller/handler pairs per window/component; do not mix service logic into controllers.
- Ensure each module/package has `__init__.py` as needed and exports intended classes.
- Keep generated files out of VCS if configured; never hand-edit generated code.

## 9) External Libraries & Docs
- Before integrating a new library/feature, review its official docs (Context7/web) and add brief notes to `docs/` if behavior affects architecture.
- Prefer existing helpers/services documented under `docs/provided-by-base/` before introducing new dependencies.

## 10) Security & Secrets
- Do not log credentials, tokens, or PII.
- Centralize configuration via `core.Config`; never hardcode secrets.

## 11) Definition of Done (UI/Feature)
- `.ui` exists and compiles cleanly.
- Controller/Handler implemented with `slot_map` and Observer wiring.
- Service code covered by tests; critical flows have integration tests.
- No direct edits to generated UI code; logging in place; exceptions typed.
- Documentation snippet added/updated in `docs/` if behavior is non-trivial.
